    "intro": "Google's SIEM replacement page opens with a bold claim: the great SIEM migration has begun. Whatever you think of the marketing, the consequence for analysts is real. If your organization moves to Google Security Operations (Google SecOps, formerly Chronicle), the tools you triage in, the language you search in and the way alerts reach you all change at once. This lesson is written for the person who will sit in that seat: Tier-1 analysts triaging alerts, Tier-2 analysts investigating and hunting, and detection engineers tuning rules.\n\nWe start by reading the migration pitch critically, converting each vendor claim into something you can test. Then we follow the data. You will learn how logs are collected and normalized into the Unified Data Model (UDM), how to read a UDM event noun by noun, and how the Entity Context Graph adds the who and what behind every IP and hostname. We move to the daily work: UDM search and raw log search with real syntax, the investigation views, and Alerts and IOC (indicator of compromise) matches.\n\nNext comes detection. You will read and write YARA-L 2.0 rules, understand Google's curated detections and risk scores, and learn how Applied Threat Intelligence prioritizes IOC matches. On the response side we cover alert grouping into cases, the Case Wall, playbooks and closing a case properly, then Gemini in Security Operations, including what it should and should not be trusted to do. A section on moving from Splunk or Sentinel shows the same detection in SPL, KQL and YARA-L side by side, and the lesson ends with a complete phishing-to-containment investigation.\n\nEvery fact here is drawn from Google's official documentation and marked where a feature is preview or package-dependent. By the end you should be able to read a UDM event, write and test a basic rule, explain why an alert landed in a case, and say precisely what you have and have not verified before closing it.",
