        "heading": "Platform Architecture for Analysts: SIEM, SOAR, Threat Intelligence and Gemini",
        "content": "Google SecOps is one platform with two halves you will see labelled throughout the console. The **SIEM** half ingests, normalizes, searches and detects. The **SOAR** half (Security Orchestration, Automation and Response) manages cases and runs playbooks. Google's architecture overview says the platform combines SIEM analytics and SOAR in a tightly coupled design and embeds threat intelligence from Google Threat Intelligence and VirusTotal plus Gemini generative AI directly into investigation and detection workflows. The SOAR product descends from Siemplify, which is why you still see actions grouped under an integration called Siemplify (for example, Assign Case and Change Case Stage).\n\n### The pipeline, stage by stage\n\n| Stage | What happens | Where you meet it |\n|---|---|---|\n| Collection | Bindplane agents, feeds, the Chronicle API and direct Google Cloud ingestion bring logs in | Settings > Data Management, Health Hub |\n| Parsing | A parser converts each raw log into a UDM event (Unified Data Model) | Search results, raw log view |\n| Enrichment | Aliasing, the Entity Context Graph and threat intelligence add context | Asset and User views, IOC matches |\n| Detection | The YARA-L engine evaluates enriched events against rules | Rules & Detections, Alerts & IOCs |\n| Prioritization | Detections become alerts with risk scores and are grouped | Alerts & IOCs, case queue |\n| Response | Cases, playbooks and manual actions | Cases, Case Wall |\n\nGoogle describes the detection engine as stateful and streaming: matching events are grouped by correlation keys across a window, a condition is evaluated when the window closes or triggers, and if it is true a detection is generated that opens or updates a case.\n\n### Data you can search, and where\n\nThe architecture page lists four search surfaces: **UDM search** over normalized events, **raw log scan** over original text, **natural language search** that uses Gemini to produce a query, and **case search** over SOAR cases and entities. SIEM Search and SOAR Search are separate pages. SIEM Search covers UDM events, alerts, raw logs, joins, statistics and case history; SOAR Search focuses on cases and entities.\n\n### Packages, as far as the documentation confirms\n\nGoogle offers three packages, all priced on ingestion volume:\n\n| Capability | Standard | Enterprise | Enterprise Plus |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Hot retention | 12 months | 12 months | 12 months |\n| Live detection rule quota | 1K single-event, 75 multi-event | 2K single-event, 200 multi-event | 3.5K single-event, 400 multi-event |\n| UEBA | Not included | Included | Included |\n| Gemini in Security Operations | Not included | Included | Included |\n| Threat intelligence | Bring your own feeds | Enriched OSINT (open-source intelligence) | Premium intelligence with Google Threat Intelligence data |\n\nRetention is 12 months by default and applies to the whole instance; it can be extended up to five years through the purchase order. The architecture page stresses there is no hot versus cold tier, so a search over eleven months should perform like a search over an hour.\n\n### Outbound-only response\n\nWhen a playbook must act inside a private network, the platform does not connect inward. A **remote agent** inside your environment polls a queue in Google Cloud over an outbound TLS connection, runs the task locally, and reports the status back. For an analyst this explains why an action against an on-premises firewall can show as pending: it waits for the agent to poll.\n\n### What this means for you\n\nKnow which half of the platform an object belongs to. A **detection** and an **alert** are SIEM objects; a **case** and its **Case Wall** are SOAR objects. When a colleague says \"the rule fired but there is no case\", the question is whether alerting was enabled and how alert grouping placed it, which later sections cover.",
