        "heading": "Ingestion: How Logs Get In, Become UDM, and Go Silent",
        "content": "Every detection and every search you run depends on one question: did the right logs arrive, parsed correctly, on time? Analysts who cannot answer that confuse \"nothing happened\" with \"nothing was collected\".\n\n### Four kinds of data\n\nGoogle SecOps distinguishes **raw logs** (the original text from the source, kept as the source of truth for forensics), **UDM events** (the normalized records parsers create from raw logs), **entity context data** (who and what a user or asset is, from sources such as Active Directory or a CMDB, a configuration management database) and **alerts** (signals from external products or from the YARA-L engine).\n\n### Ways data arrives\n\n| Method | Best for | Notes from the documentation |\n|---|---|---|\n| Direct Google Cloud ingestion | Audit, VPC Flow, DNS and firewall logs from Google Cloud | Described as the most cost-effective and performant route for Google Cloud logs |\n| Bindplane agent | On-premises Windows and Linux logs, firewalls, or cloud data you want to filter first | Based on the OpenTelemetry Collector; the legacy Forwarder is end of life |\n| Data feeds | Logs already in Cloud Storage or Amazon S3, webhook push, predefined API integrations | Log lines up to 4 MB |\n| Chronicle API | Custom or high-volume applications | The legacy Ingestion API is deprecated and will be discontinued on July 20, 2027 |\n\nIngestion supports UTF-8 only, and very large files (5 to 10 GB or more) can delay ingestion.\n\n### Log types and parsers\n\nEach source is declared with a **log type**, a label that identifies both the vendor and device and which parser converts the raw log to UDM. The documentation states there is a one-to-one relationship between a parser and a log type. Names you will meet include `WINEVTLOG` (Windows event logs), `WINDOWS_SYSMON` (Sysmon), `CS_EDR` (CrowdStrike Falcon), `PAN_FIREWALL` (Palo Alto Networks firewall), `AZURE_AD` and `GCP_CLOUDAUDIT`. A different thing entirely is `metadata.event_type` (what kind of activity the event describes): the two attributes store different information.\n\nParsers come in three forms. **Default parsers** are maintained by Google. A **customer-specific parser** replaces the default for one log type. A **parser extension** adds extra mapping instructions on top of the default without replacing it. Critically, a default parser maps a subset of core values; unmapped values stay in the raw log and are not stored in the UDM record, which is exactly when raw log search saves you.\n\n### Worked example: raw to UDM\n\nThe documentation walks through a Squid web proxy log:\n\n```\nRAW:  1588059648.129 23 192.168.23.4 TCP_HIT/200 904 GET www.google.com/images/sunlogo.png - HIER_DIRECT/203.0.113.52 image/jpeg\n\nUDM:  metadata.event_timestamp      = 2020-04-28T07:40:48.129Z   (epoch converted to RFC 3339)\n      metadata.event_type           = NETWORK_HTTP\n      metadata.vendor_name / product_name = Squid / Squid Proxy\n      principal.ip                  = 192.168.23.4               (the client)\n      target.url                    = www.google.com/images/sunlogo.png\n      target.ip                     = 203.0.113.52\n      network.http.method           = GET\n      network.http.response_code    = 200\n      security_result.action        = UNKNOWN_ACTION\n```\n\nThe content type (image/jpeg), the response time (23) and the user field were in the raw line but were not mapped in this example, so a UDM search cannot see them.\n\n### Ingestion health is part of your job\n\nThe **Health Hub** shows failed sources and failed parsers and the health of each feed, data source and log type. For hosts that stop sending, Google documents **silent-host monitoring**: Cloud Monitoring alerts on the ingestion rate per collector using ingestion labels, or a SecOps dashboard for a daily view (no alerts, with up to 6 hours of latency). Source delays also exist: blob-storage feeds are polled, so data is only as fresh as the feed frequency.\n\n### Questions to ask before trusting an absence\n\n1. Is the log type for this source reporting in the Health Hub, and is its parser healthy?\n2. What is the feed or collection interval, and could the event simply not have arrived yet?\n3. Does a raw log search (`raw = \"...\"`) find the string when UDM search does not? If yes, the parser did not map that value.",
        "codeExample": "// Raw log search: find what the parser did not map, then check whether it parsed at all\nraw = \"EncodedCommand\"\nraw = \"EncodedCommand\" and parsed = false\n\n// UDM search: normalized events only\nmetadata.log_type = \"WINDOWS_SYSMON\" and metadata.product_event_type = \"1\"\n\n// Entity and context data is NOT found with metadata.log_type in UDM search.\n// Use graph syntax instead:\ngraph.metadata.event_metadata.log_type = \"AZURE_AD_CONTEXT\""
