        "heading": "UDM in Depth: Event Types, Nouns and Reading a Normalized Event",
        "content": "The **Unified Data Model (UDM)** is Google's standard schema. Google SecOps stores every event twice: as the original raw log and as a structured UDM record. Because equivalent facts use the same field path regardless of vendor, one search or one rule can cover a Windows login, an Okta login and a Google Cloud login. Learning UDM is the single highest-value skill in this platform.\n\n### Anatomy of a UDM event\n\nEvery event has a **metadata** section (timestamps, vendor, product, log type, event type) and some of the following sections. A section with no data is simply absent.\n\n| Section | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `principal` | The acting entity that originates the activity: user, host, IP, process |\n| `target` | The entity acted upon: destination host, file, user account, URL |\n| `src` | The source object being acted upon, when different from the principal (for example, the file and machine a user copies from) |\n| `intermediary` | Devices the activity passed through, such as proxies and SMTP relays |\n| `observer` | A device that passively monitors, such as a packet sniffer or vulnerability scanner |\n| `about` | Other objects the event references, such as email attachments or DLLs loaded during a launch |\n| `network` | Protocol detail: `network.dns.questions.name`, `network.http.method`, `network.http.user_agent`, `network.direction`, bytes sent and received |\n| `security_result` | What a security product concluded: `action`, `severity`, `category`, `rule_name`, `summary`, `risk_score` |\n| `extensions` | Specialised blocks such as `extensions.auth` for authentication details (type, mechanism) |\n\nFor authentication events Google says to put the source of the login in the principal and the thing being logged into in the target. If the login goes through single sign-on, the principal is the user's device, the target is the cloud service and the intermediary is the SSO provider.\n\n### The event types you will use daily\n\n`metadata.event_type` decides which other fields must be present. Common values: `PROCESS_LAUNCH`, `PROCESS_INJECTION`, `PROCESS_MODULE_LOAD`, `NETWORK_CONNECTION`, `NETWORK_DNS`, `NETWORK_HTTP`, `USER_LOGIN`, `USER_LOGOUT`, `USER_CREATION`, `FILE_CREATION`, `FILE_MODIFICATION`, `REGISTRY_MODIFICATION`, `EMAIL_TRANSACTION`. Event types are enumerated values, so they are uppercase strings in double quotes and cannot be matched with a regular expression.\n\n### Worked example: Sysmon Event ID 1 becomes PROCESS_LAUNCH\n\nSysmon (System Monitor, a Microsoft tool that logs process creation and more) Event ID 1 records a process being created. Google's WINDOWS_SYSMON parser maps it to `PROCESS_LAUNCH`. The key trap: the **parent** process lands in `principal.process` and the **new child** process lands in `target.process`.\n\n| Raw Sysmon field | UDM field |\n|---|---|\n| EventID = 1 | `metadata.event_type = \"PROCESS_LAUNCH\"`, `metadata.product_event_type = \"1\"` |\n| Computer or Hostname | `principal.hostname` |\n| User (DOMAIN\\name) | `principal.user.userid` and `principal.administrative_domain` |\n| ParentImage | `principal.process.file.full_path` |\n| ParentCommandLine | `principal.process.command_line` |\n| ParentProcessId | `principal.process.pid` |\n| Image | `target.process.file.full_path` |\n| CommandLine | `target.process.command_line` |\n| Hashes (SHA256=...) | `target.process.file.sha256` |\n| EventID | `security_result.rule_name = \"EventID: 1\"` |\n\n### What the analyst asks\n\n- **Which noun holds the thing I care about?** To find what ran, search `target.process`; to find who or what launched it, search `principal.process`. Reversing them returns the wrong population or nothing.\n- **Which parser populated this field?** Different parsers fill different nouns for the same idea. Open one event, read its fields, then write the query.\n- **Is the field repeated?** Fields such as `principal.ip` can hold lists. Search treats them with \"any\" semantics, which matters for `!=`.\n- **Is the value there at all?** A missing field is not an error; it is an unmapped value. Check the raw log.",
        "codeExample": "// RAW (Sysmon EventID 1, JSON form, fictional host and user)\n{\n  \"EventID\": 1,\n  \"Hostname\": \"fin-wks-0231.corp.example.com\",\n  \"User\": \"CORP\\\\j.alvarez\",\n  \"Image\": \"C:\\\\Windows\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\",\n  \"CommandLine\": \"powershell.exe -nop -w hidden -enc JABjAGwAaQBlAG4AdAAgAD0AIABOAGUAdwAtAE8AYgBqAGUAYwB0AA==\",\n  \"ParentImage\": \"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\Microsoft Office\\\\root\\\\Office16\\\\WINWORD.EXE\",\n  \"ParentCommandLine\": \"\\\"WINWORD.EXE\\\" /n \\\"C:\\\\Users\\\\j.alvarez\\\\Downloads\\\\Invoice_4471.docm\\\"\",\n  \"ParentProcessId\": \"7120\",\n  \"Hashes\": \"SHA256=9F86D081884C7D659A2FEAA0C55AD015A3BF4F1B2B0B822CD15D6C15B0F00A08\"\n}\n\n// UDM (fields the WINDOWS_SYSMON parser maps for EventID 1)\nmetadata.log_type                 = \"WINDOWS_SYSMON\"\nmetadata.event_type               = \"PROCESS_LAUNCH\"\nmetadata.product_event_type       = \"1\"\nprincipal.hostname                = \"fin-wks-0231.corp.example.com\"\nprincipal.user.userid             = \"j.alvarez\"\nprincipal.administrative_domain   = \"CORP\"\nprincipal.process.pid             = \"7120\"\nprincipal.process.file.full_path  = \"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\Microsoft Office\\\\root\\\\Office16\\\\WINWORD.EXE\"\nprincipal.process.command_line    = \"\\\"WINWORD.EXE\\\" /n \\\"C:\\\\Users\\\\j.alvarez\\\\Downloads\\\\Invoice_4471.docm\\\"\"\ntarget.process.file.full_path     = \"C:\\\\Windows\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\"\ntarget.process.command_line       = \"powershell.exe -nop -w hidden -enc JABjAGwAaQBlAG4AdAAgAD0AIABOAGUAdwAtAE8AYgBqAGUAYwB0AA==\"\nsecurity_result.rule_name         = \"EventID: 1\"\n\n// Search that finds it (note: powershell is the TARGET, Word is the PRINCIPAL)\nmetadata.event_type = \"PROCESS_LAUNCH\"\nprincipal.process.file.full_path = /winword\\.exe$/ nocase\ntarget.process.file.full_path = /powershell\\.exe$/ nocase"
