      {
        "heading": "Entity Context and the Entity Context Graph: Who and What Is Behind the Event",
        "content": "A UDM event tells you that user abc launched shady.exe. It does not tell you that abc was terminated last week, or that the machine hosts finance data. Google's documentation uses exactly this example to explain **entities**: records that describe the \"who\" and \"what\" of your environment so an event can be judged in context.\n\n### Events versus entities\n\nA **UDM event** is point-in-time telemetry: something happened. A **UDM entity** is a state-in-time description of an asset, user or resource, taken from an authoritative source. Entities are stored in the **Entity Context Graph (ECG)**, a graph database that holds stateful records of assets, users and threat indicators so they can be correlated with events in search, rules and dashboards.\n\n### Where entity data comes from\n\n| Context | Source | Examples |\n|---|---|---|\n| Entity context | Customer-provided, ingested | Active Directory, Azure AD, Okta, Google Cloud Identity, ServiceNow CMDB, vulnerability scanners |\n| Derived context | Generated by Google SecOps from your activity | Prevalence, first-seen time and last-seen time for each entity |\n| Global context | Google-sourced | Google Threat Intelligence data, Safe Browsing lists, WHOIS |\n\nPrevalence is how often an entity occurs in your UDM data compared with other entities. First-seen and last-seen answer \"is this new to our environment?\". The ECG creates entity-context data with a five-day look-back window to handle late-arriving data.\n\n### Merging and aliasing are different things\n\nGoogle is explicit: **UDM aliasing** enriches an event by adding identifiers, for example mapping an IP address to a hostname inside that event; **ECG merging** combines context from several sources into one entity profile. The ECG merges records that share a key such as hostname, MAC address, user ID or email address. If user jdoe appears in Okta, Azure AD and a vulnerability scanner, the ECG produces one unified user entity with attributes from all three.\n\nAsset aliasing uses DHCP (Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol) events to link hostnames, MAC addresses and IP addresses, EDR data to map product IDs to hostnames, and asset context records to attach software version and deployment status. Because an IP address belongs to different machines at different times, aliasing is time-aware.\n\n### Time is part of the answer\n\nWhen an entity attribute changes, the ECG keeps the old value for the interval in which it was valid. A search spanning the change can return more than one context record for the same entity. When you read an Asset view, always confirm the timestamp you entered: the answer to \"which host had 10.20.30.41\" depends on when.\n\n### Worked example: using prevalence in a rule\n\nGoogle's own IOC rule joins a DNS event with entity data. The relevant lines:\n\n```\n$e.metadata.event_type = \"NETWORK_DNS\"\n$e.network.dns.questions.name = $hostname\n$p.graph.metadata.entity_type = \"DOMAIN_NAME\"\n$p.graph.entity.domain.prevalence.rolling_max > 0\n$p.graph.entity.domain.prevalence.rolling_max <= 3\n$p.graph.entity.domain.name = $hostname\n```\n\nTranslation: a host looked up a domain that very few assets in your environment have ever contacted. Low prevalence on its own is weak evidence, since new software and new vendors are rare too. Combined with an IOC match (the original rule adds one) it becomes a strong lead.\n\n### Questions an analyst asks of context\n\n1. Is this user active, terminated, privileged or a service account? Which department and manager?\n2. Is this asset a workstation or a crown-jewel server, and what is its owner and criticality?\n3. How prevalent is this domain, file or process in our environment? When was it first seen?\n4. Did the context source ingest recently enough to be trusted, or is the ECG missing this entity?\n\n### Searching entities\n\nEntity data is searched with graph syntax, not with `metadata.log_type`. For example `graph.entity.hostname = \"fin-wks-0231.corp.example.com\"` returns entity records for that asset. If a context source seems absent, check its ingestion in the Health Hub first."
      },
      {
        "heading": "UDM Search and Raw Log Search: Syntax, Hunts and Mistakes",
        "content": "You find things on the **SIEM Search** page. Google's current search documentation states that the query language across search, dashboards and rules is **YARA-L 2.0**, with a query editor that accepts the YL2 dialect (default) or SQL (GoogleSQL). Existing UDM queries keep working: a search condition is just a UDM field, an operator and a value.\n\n### Syntax you must know\n\n| Need | Syntax |\n|---|---|\n| Exact string | `principal.hostname = \"fin-wks-0231\"` |\n| Case-insensitive | `principal.user.userid = \"J.Alvarez\" nocase` (string comparison is case-sensitive by default) |\n| Regular expression (RE2) | `target.process.command_line = /\\bpsexec(\\.exe)?\\b/ nocase` |\n| Enumerations | `metadata.event_type = \"NETWORK_CONNECTION\"` (uppercase, quoted, no regex) |\n| Numbers | `target.port = 443` and `network.sent_bytes > 10485760` (no quotes) |\n| Logic | AND, OR, NOT with parentheses; AND is assumed if omitted |\n| Custom key-value | `additional.fields[\"pod_name\"] = \"kube-scheduler-prod\"` |\n| Grouped fields | `ip = \"198.51.100.77\"` searches every IP-bearing noun at once (slower) |\n| Raw logs | `raw = \"EncodedCommand\"` or `raw = /regex/`, optionally `parsed = false` |\n| Comments | `//` and `/* ... */` |\n\nBackslashes and quotes must be escaped: `\"C:\\\\Windows\\\\System32\\\\net.exe\"`. Avoid starting a regex with a wildcard such as `/.*pattern/`; it blocks index pruning and forces a slow scan.\n\n### Time, limits and modes\n\nThe maximum search range depends on type: 365 days for UDM and raw, 90 days for statistics, and shorter for joins. The default maximum result count is 30k. Basic Mode (fast event retrieval) and Advanced Mode (timeline, prevalence scores, correlated detections) are described as preview features, so check your tenant. **UDM Lookup** helps when you do not know the field name: it searches field names or stored values.\n\n### Worked hunts\n\n```\n// 1. Encoded PowerShell launched by an Office application\nmetadata.event_type = \"PROCESS_LAUNCH\"\nprincipal.process.file.full_path = /\\\\(winword|excel|outlook|powerpnt)\\.exe$/ nocase\ntarget.process.file.full_path = /\\\\powershell\\.exe$/ nocase\ntarget.process.command_line = /\\s-(e|ec|en|enc|encodedcommand)\\s+\\S{40,}/ nocase\n\n// 2. Rare domains: how many distinct hosts asked for each domain (statistics search)\nmetadata.event_type = \"NETWORK_DNS\"\n$domain = network.dns.questions.name\n$domain != \"\"\nmatch:\n  $domain\noutcome:\n  $hosts = count_distinct(principal.hostname)\n  $queries = count(metadata.id)\norder:\n  $hosts asc\nlimit:\n  50\n\n// 3. Possible impossible travel: one user, two cities within an hour\nmetadata.event_type = \"USER_LOGIN\"\nprincipal.user.userid = $user\nprincipal.location.city = $city\nmatch:\n  $user over 1h\ncondition:\n  #city > 1\n```\n\nSearch 2 returns one row per domain. A domain asked for by exactly one host is not malicious by itself; it is a lead to open in the Domain view. Search 3 is a heuristic: VPNs and mobile carriers create false positives, so confirm with the IP and device before escalating.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Principal versus target.** Searching `principal.process` when the process you want is the child returns nothing. See the previous sections.\n- **Regex on an enumeration.** `metadata.event_type = /NETWORK_*/` is invalid; list values with OR.\n- **The `!=` trap on repeated fields.** `principal.ip != \"1.2.3.4\"` matches an event that also contains other IPs.\n- **Search versus rule counts.** A search returns every matching event; a live rule deduplicates detections across adjacent windows and treats repeated fields differently. Reconcile before you say a rule \"missed\" something.\n- **Old events, new ingest.** An event with an old timestamp ingested today only appears if your time range covers its event time.\n- **Assuming UDM sees everything.** Unmapped values live only in the raw log, so fall back to `raw =`.",
        "codeExample": "// Trim output to the columns you need (select) - faster and easier to read\nmetadata.event_type = \"NETWORK_HTTP\" and principal.hostname != \"\"\nselect:\n  principal.hostname, target.url, network.http.user_agent\n\n// Statistics search: failed logins per user per day, highest first\nmetadata.event_type = \"USER_LOGIN\"\nsecurity_result.action = \"FAIL\"\n$user = principal.user.userid\n$user != \"\"\nmatch:\n  $user by day\noutcome:\n  $daily_failed = count(metadata.id)\norder:\n  $daily_failed desc\nlimit:\n  25\n\n// Raw log search for a value the parser did not map, limited to unparsed logs\nraw = \"Invoice_4471.docm\" and parsed = false"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Investigation Views, Alerts and IOC Matches",
        "content": "Search is flexible, but a Tier-1 analyst often starts from an object: an alert, a host, a user, an IP address, a domain or a file hash. Google SecOps gives each a dedicated **investigation view** that pre-assembles the relevant timeline and context. Google's own documentation notes that UDM search is more capable than the views for deep work, so treat views as the fast first look and search as the follow-up.\n\n### The views\n\n| View | You enter | You get |\n|---|---|---|\n| Alert view | An alert | Details, status and related entities of one alert |\n| Asset view | Hostname, internal IP or MAC, plus a timestamp | Timeline for that asset, domains it contacted, first and last seen, and the IP and MAC it had at that time. It looks from inside your enterprise outward |\n| User view | A username or email address | User information from sources such as Active Directory, Workday or Okta, with aliasing so that dennis and dennis@altostrat.com return the same events; the timeline moves in one-week steps |\n| IP address view | An IP address | Prevalence and activity for that IP. It starts outside your enterprise and looks in |\n| Domain view | A domain or URL | References in your logs plus WHOIS, prevalence and VirusTotal context (VT Context) |\n| File hash view | An MD5, SHA-1 or SHA-256 | Partner engines detecting the file, properties and VirusTotal filenames |\n\nIn Asset view the default activity window is 2 hours, adjustable between one minute and one day with a time slider. Asset view does not return events of type GENERIC_EVENT, so some events can be missing from it that search would show.\n\n### Asset insight blocks\n\nAsset view highlights domains worth a second look:\n\n- **Newly registered domains**: registered within 30 days before the start of the window, using WHOIS data. Fresh domains are a common attacker choice.\n- **Domains new to the enterprise**: never queried by anyone in your company before.\n- **Low prevalence domains**: the top private domain has prevalence of 10 or less.\n- **Forwarded alerts** from your existing security tools, plus intelligence lists such as the Emerging Threats Intelligence Rep List and the US DHS Automated Indicator Sharing feed.\n\nThe **Prevalence graph** plots first connections as large circles and repeat connections as small ones; rare domains rise to the top and red triangles mark alerts.\n\n### Alerts and IOC matches\n\n**Detection > Alerts & IOCs** has two tabs. The **Alerts** tab lists alerts, defined as rule detections with alerting enabled (or alerts ingested from other products), showing severity, priority, risk score and verdict. The **IOC Matches** tab lists indicators of compromise (IOCs: domains, IP addresses, file hashes, URLs) seen in your data and matched against threat intelligence feeds, with confidence score, severity, feed name and category.\n\nHow matching works, per Google's documentation:\n\n- Data is normalized to UDM, then the matching engine compares UDM events against indicators from all configured feeds.\n- **Real-time matching** checks new telemetry as it arrives.\n- **Retroactive matching** scans historical data, up to your full retention period (typically one year), whenever a new indicator is added to a feed.\n- Matching focuses on domains and IP addresses; fields used include `network.dns.questions` and `principal.ip`, plus file hashes (MD5, SHA-1, SHA-256) in fields such as `principal.process.file`.\n\nSo an alert fired today can describe activity from months ago, because the intelligence arrived late. That is a feature, and it changes your question from \"is this happening now\" to \"when did this start\".\n\n### Worked example: first five minutes on an IOC match\n\n```\nAlert:   IOC match, domain cdn-update-service.example\n1. Which asset? -> open Asset view at the event time (not \"now\").\n2. What did it do around that moment? -> read the timeline 15 minutes either side.\n3. Was the domain new to the enterprise or low prevalence? -> check insight blocks.\n4. Was it one query or a repeating pattern? -> search network.dns.questions.name over 30 days.\n5. What ran just before? -> pivot to PROCESS_LAUNCH events for that hostname.\n6. Verdict: benign, suspicious (escalate with the timeline attached) or confirmed.\n```\n\n### Pitfalls\n\n- Opening Asset view at the wrong time and concluding \"no activity\".\n- Treating an IOC match as proof of compromise. A match means the indicator appeared; you still decide whether the connection succeeded and whether the host actually ran something malicious."
      },
      {
        "heading": "YARA-L 2.0 Detection Rules: Reading and Writing Them",
        "content": "A **rule** is a saved YARA-L query that the detection engine evaluates continuously. Even if you never write one, as a Tier-2 analyst you must read the rule behind an alert to know why it fired and whether it is wrong. YARA-L is the language of Google SecOps search, dashboards and rules alike, and its name nods to YARA, the malware pattern-matching tool.\n\n### The sections\n\n| Order | Section | Rules | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `meta` | Required | Key-value details: author, description, severity |\n| 2 | `events` | Required | Declares event variables like `$e` and filters them with UDM fields; declares placeholder variables like `$user` |\n| 3 | `match` | Needed for multi-event rules | Group-by keys plus a time window |\n| 4 | `outcome` | Optional | Computed values such as `count($e.metadata.id)` and the special `$risk_score` |\n| 5 | `condition` | Required | Trigger logic such as `#fail >= 5 and #ok >= 1` |\n| 6 | `options` | Optional | `allow_zero_values`, `suppression_window` |\n\nA **single-event rule** has no `match` section, or checks only for the existence of one event. A **multi-event rule** groups events by placeholder values inside a window and counts or correlates them; Google applies lower live-rule quotas to these, so write single-event rules when no correlation is needed.\n\n### Placeholders, windows and counts\n\nA **placeholder variable** such as `$user = $fail.target.user.userid` captures a value and joins event variables: using the same placeholder on two events means \"same user\". Every event variable in a rule must be joined to every other. In `match`, the window can be a hop window (`$user over 10m`), a tumbling window (`$user by 1h`) or a sliding window anchored on a pivot event (`over 5m after $e1`). In rules the window ranges from 1 minute to 48 hours. In `condition`, `#e` is the number of distinct events matching `$e`, while `$e` alone means \"at least one\". Unbounded conditions such as `!$e2` detect the absence of an event and add a one-hour delay for late data.\n\nTwo safety rules: filter out zero values (omitted fields default to empty strings, so two empty fields can falsely \"match\" each other), and put cheap equality filters, especially the event type, first so the engine evaluates fewer events.\n\n### Reference lists and risk score\n\n`$e.target.user.userid in %privileged_accounts` tests membership in a **reference list** (a managed list of values, written with a percent prefix). In `outcome`, `$risk_score` sets the alert's score; if you omit it, alerting rules default to 40 and non-alerting detections to 15. Google recommends bands: 90 to 100 active compromise beyond one endpoint, 80 to 89 single-endpoint compromise, 50 to 79 potential issue needing investigation.\n\n### A second rule, single-event\n\n```\nrule encoded_powershell_from_office {\n  meta:\n    author = \"SOC Detection Engineering\"\n    description = \"Encoded PowerShell launched by an Office application\"\n    severity = \"HIGH\"\n  events:\n    $e.metadata.event_type = \"PROCESS_LAUNCH\"\n    $e.principal.process.file.full_path = /\\\\(winword|excel|outlook|powerpnt)\\.exe$/ nocase\n    $e.target.process.file.full_path = /\\\\powershell\\.exe$/ nocase\n    $e.target.process.command_line = /\\s-(e|ec|en|enc|encodedcommand)\\s+\\S{40,}/ nocase\n    $e.principal.hostname = $hostname\n  outcome:\n    $risk_score = 85\n    $suspect_host = $hostname\n  condition:\n    $e\n}\n```\n\nThe regex requires whitespace after the switch and a long token, so `-ExecutionPolicy Bypass` does not match.\n\n### Rules Editor, testing and rollout\n\nIn the Rules Editor you write and save a rule, use **Run test** against historical data, then enable the **Live Rule** toggle so it evaluates incoming data. A live rule only produces alerts if **alerting** is also on. A **retrohunt** applies the rule to history: run it with alerting disabled, because a retrohunt on an alerting rule creates a SOAR alert for every detection and can flood the case queue. Each rule version is treated as new logic and can re-detect past events. Overly heavy live rules move to Limited and then Paused status.\n\nFor rules with a `match` section the engine deduplicates detections with identical match values in adjacent windows, and `suppression_window` lets you throttle further.",
        "codeExample": "rule okta_bruteforce_then_success {\n  meta:\n    author = \"SOC Detection Engineering\"\n    description = \"Five or more failed Okta logins, then a success, for the same user and source IP within 10 minutes\"\n    severity = \"HIGH\"\n    tactic = \"Credential Access\"\n  events:\n    $fail.metadata.vendor_name = \"Okta\"\n    $fail.metadata.event_type = \"USER_LOGIN\"\n    $fail.security_result.action = \"FAIL\"\n    $fail.target.user.userid = $user\n    $fail.principal.ip = $ip\n\n    $ok.metadata.vendor_name = \"Okta\"\n    $ok.metadata.event_type = \"USER_LOGIN\"\n    $ok.security_result.action = \"ALLOW\"\n    $ok.target.user.userid = $user\n    $ok.principal.ip = $ip\n\n    // the success must come after the failures\n    $fail.metadata.event_timestamp.seconds < $ok.metadata.event_timestamp.seconds\n  match:\n    $user, $ip over 10m\n  outcome:\n    $fail_count = count_distinct($fail.metadata.id)\n    $risk_score = 85\n  condition:\n    #fail >= 5 and #ok >= 1\n}"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Curated Detections, Risk Analytics and Tuning",
        "content": "You will not write every rule. Google's Cloud Threat Intelligence (GCTI) team provides and maintains **curated detections**: YARA-L rules grouped into **rule sets** that identify threats in your ingested data, delivered under the \"shared fate\" model. Most alerts a Tier-1 analyst sees in a new deployment come from these sets or from IOC matching, so understanding how they are switched on and tuned is core analyst knowledge.\n\n### Rule sets, categories and what they need\n\nRule sets are organised into categories. Google documents Cloud Threats, Chrome Enterprise Threats, Windows Threats, Linux Threats, macOS Threats, Risk Analytics for UEBA and Applied Threat Intelligence. The Windows Threats category, for example, includes Anomalous PowerShell, Hacktool, Info Stealer, Ransomware, RAT (remote access tool), Living off the Land Binaries, Security Posture Downgrade and Mandiant Front-Line Threats. Those rule sets are tested with EDR (endpoint detection and response) sources such as Microsoft Sysmon (`WINDOWS_SYSMON`), CrowdStrike Falcon (`CS_EDR`), Carbon Black (`CB_EDR`) and SentinelOne, and expect fields such as principal and target process path, command line and hash. If the fields are not mapped, the rules cannot fire. Google offers **Managed Detection Testing** test rules to confirm the data arrives in the right format.\n\n### Precise versus broad\n\nEach rule set can have two kinds of rules, shown on the Curated Detections page under Rules:\n\n| Kind | Meaning | Typical use |\n|---|---|---|\n| Precise | Finds malicious behavior with higher confidence and fewer false positives | Alerting on |\n| Broad | Finds suspicious or anomalous behavior that is more common and noisier | Enabled for visibility, often alerting off |\n\nTwo independent toggles control a set: **Status** (Enabled or Disabled) decides whether the rules generate detections, and **Alerting** (On or Off) decides whether detections also generate alerts. A set can be enabled with alerting off to measure volume before it reaches the queue. By default new rule sets arrive Disabled with alerting Off, except Applied Threat Intelligence sets, which are automatically enabled for Enterprise Plus instances. New rules added to an existing set inherit that set's settings for their precision level.\n\n### Tuning with rule exclusions\n\nCurated rules cannot be edited by you; only Google can change them. To reduce noise you create **rule exclusions** that stop matching events from being evaluated by a rule set or by specific rules. Exclusions are used only with curated detections, not with your own rules. Documented example fields include `metadata.product_event_type`, `principal.user.userid`, `principal.ip` and `network.http.user_agent`.\n\n```\nScenario: Anomalous PowerShell fires every morning on the IT automation account.\nWrong:  disable the whole rule set.\nRight:  1. Confirm it is the approved patch script (ticket, owner, hash).\n        2. Add an exclusion scoped to that rule set on principal.user.userid = \"svc-patching\".\n        3. Re-check the dashboard in a week: did volume drop, and did anything real disappear?\n```\n\nCalling approved admin activity a \"false positive\" is imprecise: the rule did what it was built to do. Document the reason so a reviewer can tell tuning from blinding.\n\n### Risk Analytics and composite detections\n\nEvery detection and alert carries a **risk score**. **Risk Analytics** associates those findings with the entities involved and shows entities by risk. Normalized scores run from 1 to 1000, and weighting defaults to 0.2 for how alert and detection scores contribute to an entity's base score. It is available with the Enterprise and Enterprise Plus packages. Rules can alert on a risk change using the `ENTITY_RISK_CHANGE` event type, for example `$e1.extensions.entity_risk.risk_score >= 100`. **Composite detections** take other rules' detections as input to build one high-fidelity alert, such as a malware-download detection followed by C2 (command-and-control) beaconing from the same host.\n\n### Questions to ask when a curated alert lands\n\n1. Which rule set and which kind (precise or broad) fired, and which MITRE tactic does it map to?\n2. Which entity carries the risk and what else has fired on it recently?\n3. Is the activity approved, and is a tuning exclusion justified?"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Applied Threat Intelligence: Prioritising IOC Matches Like a Tier-1 Analyst",
        "content": "A threat intelligence feed is a list of indicators of compromise (IOCs): domains, IP addresses, file hashes and URLs known to be associated with attackers. The hard part has never been getting the list; it is knowing which match deserves your next ten minutes. **Applied Threat Intelligence (ATI)** is Google's answer: it continually evaluates your telemetry against IOCs curated by Google Threat Intelligence (GTI), which unifies Google, Mandiant and VirusTotal intelligence. Google's marketing page frames this as early warning for novel threats seen by Mandiant incident responders on the front lines.\n\n### Availability\n\nATI in Google SecOps requires an Enterprise Plus license. Under the packages table, Enterprise includes enriched open-source intelligence, and Enterprise Plus adds premium intelligence from GTI data. For Enterprise Plus instances the ATI curated rule sets are enabled by default; review their Status and Alerting on the Curated Detections page. Google is also migrating from legacy Mandiant feeds to the unified GTI feed.\n\n### From IOC match to prioritised alert\n\nWhen ATI is enabled, Google SecOps ingests IOCs that the **Google Threat Indicator (GTI) verdict** classifies as malicious. A raw IOC match says only \"this string appeared\". ATI alerts are matches contextualised by YARA-L curated rules using Mandiant intelligence, so each alert carries a **priority**. The features behind the priority, per Google:\n\n| Feature | What it tells you |\n|---|---|\n| GTI Verdict and GTI Severity | Google's unified judgement and calculated severity |\n| Active IR | Sourced from an active incident response engagement |\n| Prevalence | Commonly observed by Mandiant |\n| Attribution | Strongly associated with a threat tracked by Mandiant |\n| Blocked | Whether your controls already blocked the indicator |\n| Network direction | Inbound or outbound traffic |\n\nThe priority models, from most to least urgent:\n\n| Model | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Active breach | Indicators Mandiant observed in active or past compromises, with a Malicious verdict and High severity |\n| High | Not seen in Mandiant investigations but associated by GTI with threat actors or malware; network indicators match outbound traffic only |\n| Medium | Malicious verdict and High severity, even if not seen in investigations; network indicators match outbound traffic only. Google recommends keeping Medium packs in non-alerting mode because of volume |\n| Inbound IP authentication | IP addresses that authenticate to your infrastructure inbound; requires the UDM authentication extension in the event |\n\nAn **Emerging Threats Center** (Enterprise Plus) also uses GTI and Gemini to show campaigns (large coordinated attacks against many organisations) and operations (focused missions against one organisation) that may affect you.\n\n### How a Tier-1 analyst uses it\n\n```\nAlert: ATI priority = Active breach, indicator = domain, host = fin-wks-0231\nStep 1  Direction and success: was it an outbound DNS lookup only, or also a connection (NETWORK_CONNECTION to the resolved IP)?\nStep 2  Blocked?  If the proxy or DNS filter blocked it, the host asked but never connected. Still investigate why it asked.\nStep 3  What launched the request? Pivot to PROCESS_LAUNCH on the host within minutes before the first query.\nStep 4  Look at the indicator itself: IOC Matches details (association, campaign, GTI rationale) and VT Context in the Domain view.\nStep 5  Time: because matching is retroactive, search 12 months for the same domain to find earlier victims.\nStep 6  Decide: escalate with the timeline, or close with the reason recorded.\n```\n\n### Limits worth remembering\n\n- An IOC match is evidence, not a verdict. A shared hosting IP can host benign sites as well as malicious ones.\n- Priority is Google's model, not yours. A Medium match on your domain controller deserves more attention than a High match on a lab VM you already isolated.\n- Intelligence changes. An IOC match can appear later for old activity, so a \"new alert about last quarter\" means a retroactive match, not a delayed log.\n- Google does not share your security data with VirusTotal when it compares your data to VirusTotal's malware database; context is brought to you, not sent out."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Cases and SOAR: From Alert to Playbook to Closed Case",
        "content": "The SIEM raises alerts; the SOAR half turns them into work. A **case** is the unit an analyst owns: one or more related alerts, the entities involved, the actions taken and the discussion, with a full audit trail. Google's documentation describes alerts arriving with their events and key indicators, which are extracted and represented as **entities**: persistent objects that collect enrichment data, analyst comments and historical context so you can track one IP, user or host over time and across cases.\n\n### How alerts become cases: alert grouping\n\nAlerts are grouped into cases by configurable **grouping rules** (Settings > Investigation > Alert Grouping). Important behaviors from Google's documentation:\n\n- Grouping happens only on open alerts and cases; it does not reopen closed cases (unless the option to reopen when grouped by source grouping identifier is enabled).\n- The general settings cap the alerts per case (30 in the documentation) and set a grouping timeframe of 0.5 to 24 hours.\n- Rules are matched in a fixed hierarchy: Alert Type, then Product, then Data Source, then the fallback rule. Once a rule matches, the system stops checking.\n- Grouping by **entities** needs only one matching entity. Two alerts sharing a source IP are grouped even if the destination and username differ.\n- A **Don't Group** rule keeps an alert in its own case.\n\nWhy you care: if one case holds 25 alerts, one noisy entity may be dragging unrelated alerts together; if a single incident is split across five cases, the grouping rule or blocklist needs review.\n\n### Working a case\n\n| Area | What you do there |\n|---|---|\n| Case queue | Active cases refreshed every minute, in default, compact or list views; sort, filter and bulk close |\n| Case Overview tab | Widgets set by the admin: Alerts, Insights, Entities highlights, Pending Actions, Case description, Gemini Summary |\n| Case Wall tab | Chronological log of actions, status changes, tasks, comments, pinned chat messages and attachments up to 50 MB per file, filterable by activity tags |\n| Alert Overview and playbook tabs | Alert events and which playbook ran |\n| Entity Explorer | Details and enrichment attributes for one entity |\n| Manual Actions | Run an integration action, such as an enrichment, on a selected entity |\n\n### Playbooks\n\nA **playbook** is a set of steps that collects information from internal and external sources, decides how to handle the alert and can act on remote systems, for example blocking a firewall port or disabling an Active Directory user. Building blocks:\n\n- **Triggers** attach a playbook automatically. For alert scope the options include Alert Type, Product Name, Network Name, Tag Name, Custom Trigger and All; for case scope they include All, Custom Trigger and Tag Name.\n- **Actions** belong to an **integration** (VirusTotal, an EDR product, an identity provider) and return messages, tables, JSON and insights to the Case Wall. **Placeholders** such as an entity identifier fill action parameters at run time.\n- **Blocks** are reusable mini-playbooks; changing a block updates every playbook that uses it.\n- **Manual actions** assign a step to a user or SOC role, with an optional **Time to respond** deadline; the playbook pauses until someone acts, and the step appears in Pending Actions.\n- Actions inside private networks run through the outbound-polling **remote agent**.\n\n### Closing a case\n\nYou close a case from the case page, selecting a **reason** and then a **root cause** (the reason must be chosen first), plus optional comments that post to the Case Wall. For 1 to 50 cases use bulk close in the queue or Search; above 50, use the Close Cases Based On Search job in the Tools integration, with a custom fixed time range so you do not accidentally close newly ingested cases. Playbook actions can also close cases automatically.\n\n### Worked case\n\n```\n08:04  Alert grouped: \"Encoded PowerShell from Office\" on fin-wks-0231 (rule fired, risk score 85)\n08:05  Playbook runs: enrich host and user, query VirusTotal for the domain and hash\n08:06  Pending Action to Tier-2 role: \"Isolate host?\" (Time to respond 15 min)\n08:11  Analyst reviews Insights and Case Wall, approves isolation; action result Completed\n08:30  Analyst adds entity comment and closes: reason = Malicious, root cause = Phishing   (the available values are set by your admin)\n```",
        "codeExample": "flowchart TD\n  A[\"Rule detection with alerting on, or IOC match, or alert from another product\"] --> B[\"Alert ingested with events and entities\"]\n  B --> C{\"Alert grouping rule matches an open case?\"}\n  C -->|\"Yes: shared entity or source grouping identifier\"| D[\"Alert added to existing case\"]\n  C -->|\"No\"| E[\"New case created\"]\n  D --> F[\"Playbook triggers attach: Alert Type, Product Name, Tag, Custom\"]\n  E --> F\n  F --> G[\"Automated actions: enrich entities, query VirusTotal, add insights\"]\n  G --> H{\"Manual action required?\"}\n  H -->|\"Yes\"| I[\"Pending Action assigned to user or SOC role\"]\n  H -->|\"No\"| J[\"Analyst reviews Case Overview and Case Wall\"]\n  I --> J\n  J --> K[\"Close case: reason, root cause, comments\"]"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Gemini in Security Operations: Fast Drafts, Verified by You",
        "content": "Gemini is Google's family of large language models (LLMs). In Google SecOps it is built into specific workflows rather than offered as a general chatbot. The platform documentation lists the features it powers; the Gemini capabilities are part of the Enterprise and Enterprise Plus packages, not Standard. As an analyst, treat Gemini as a fast, tireless junior colleague: useful, often right, and never the last line of verification.\n\n### What Gemini does, per the documentation\n\n| Feature | What it does for you |\n|---|---|\n| Natural language search | You type English such as \"failed user logins over the last 3 days\"; Gemini generates a query in the YARA-L 2.0 dialect and adjusts the time range |\n| Rule generation | A prompt in the Gemini pane produces a rule; \"Open in rule editor\" lets you modify it. Only single-event rules can be created this way |\n| Threat intelligence questions | Summaries about threat actors, IOCs and campaigns |\n| Documentation summaries | Answers about how Google SecOps works, drawn from the documentation |\n| Playbook creation and editing | Build and change playbooks with prompts |\n| Case summary widget | AI summary on the Case Overview with three parts: What Actually Happened?, Case Activity, and Next and Pending Actions |\n| Case and alert questions | Ask about the current case, alert or attached playbooks from the Gemini pane (documented as a preview feature) |\n| Triage and Investigation Agent (TIN) | Investigates alerts and judges true or false positive with an explanation |\n\nGoogle states that Gemini keeps conversation history privately and that user history is never used to train models. Gemini data is processed with global endpoints on Vertex AI.\n\n### The Triage and Investigation Agent\n\nTIN is the agentic feature. According to its documentation, it evaluates an incoming alert, executes an investigation plan using dynamic search queries, Google Threat Intelligence enrichment, command-line analysis and process tree reconstruction, then returns a structured explanation with findings and reasoning. Facts to know: automatic investigation is on by default for administrators who opted in; it starts five minutes after the alert by default (configurable up to 20 minutes) so related events can arrive; an investigation averages about 60 seconds and runs for at most 20 minutes; it runs only on data ingested by Google SecOps SIEM and not on alerts from SOAR connectors; and it supports only specific log types and single-tenant style deployments. The architecture overview describes a loop in which confirmed true positives trigger response playbooks and false positives close automatically. Find out what your own tenant actually enables and who audits the closures.\n\n### Discipline: verify what Gemini produces\n\n```\nPrompt:  all activity from winword.exe on lab-pc\nQuery Gemini might return (illustrative):\n  principal.hostname = \"lab-pc\" AND\n  (principal.process.file.full_path = /winword\\.exe/ nocase OR\n   target.process.file.full_path = /winword\\.exe/ nocase)\n\nYour checks before trusting the result:\n  1. Read every field. Principal or target? Did it pick the noun you meant?\n  2. Is the time range what you asked for? Gemini adjusts it from words like \"yesterday\".\n  3. Is hostname = \"lab-pc\" an exact match when the real host is lab-pc.corp.example.com?\n  4. Compare the count with a simpler hand-written query.\n  5. For a generated rule: Run test, check false-positive volume, never enable alerting untested.\n```\n\n### Where Gemini helps and where it can mislead\n\n- **Good:** drafting a first query when you do not remember field names, explaining an encoded command line, summarising a long case for handover, turning an idea into rule skeleton.\n- **Risky:** accepting a case summary without checking entities (the summary does not regenerate automatically when entity enrichment updates; refresh it), trusting a one-line verdict about a true or false positive, or enabling a generated rule as-is.\n- **Good habit:** write down in the Case Wall which conclusions were Gemini-suggested and which you verified.\n\nThe accountable analyst remains you. A generated query that returns zero results means either nothing happened or the query is wrong, and only you can tell which."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Migrating From a Legacy SIEM: What Changes at Your Desk",
        "content": "Most analysts joining a Google SecOps shop come from Splunk, QRadar or Microsoft Sentinel. The concepts transfer (ingest, normalize, search, correlate, alert, case) but the vocabulary and the mechanics differ. Google's guide \"Transition from SPL to YARA-L 2.0\" opens with the key point: SPL (Splunk Search Processing Language) is procedural, with commands piped one into the next, whereas YARA-L is declarative and section-based.\n\n### Concept map\n\n| SPL or KQL idea | Google SecOps equivalent |\n|---|---|\n| `search`, `where` on raw fields | `events` section, filtering on UDM fields |\n| `eval` | `outcome` section |\n| `stats ... by` | `match` (the group-by keys) plus `outcome` (the aggregates) |\n| `where count > 5` after stats | `condition` section |\n| `dedup` | `dedup` section (search and dashboards only) |\n| `table`, `fields` | `select` and `unselect` |\n| `sort`, `head` | `order` and `limit` |\n| Static search window (last 60m) | `match` with `over` (rules) or `by` (search), a time window defined in the query |\n| `join`, `transaction` | Multiple event variables joined by placeholder variables |\n| Per-product data models (Splunk Common Information Model, Sentinel Advanced Security Information Model) | One UDM for every source |\n\n### Side by side: failed logins\n\n```\nSPL (Splunk):\nindex=auth action=failure\n| stats count by user, src_ip\n| where count > 5\n\nKQL (Microsoft Sentinel):\nSigninLogs\n| where ResultType != \"0\"\n| summarize failures = count() by UserPrincipalName, IPAddress, bin(TimeGenerated, 10m)\n| where failures > 5\n\nYARA-L (Google SecOps rule):\nrule failed_logins_per_user_ip {\n  meta:\n    author = \"SOC Detection Engineering\"\n    severity = \"MEDIUM\"\n  events:\n    $e.metadata.event_type = \"USER_LOGIN\"\n    $e.security_result.action = \"FAIL\"\n    $user = $e.target.user.userid\n    $ip = $e.principal.ip\n  match:\n    $user, $ip over 10m\n  outcome:\n    $failures = count($e.metadata.id)\n  condition:\n    $failures > 5\n}\n```\n\nNotice three differences. The YARA-L version is **continuous**: a window slides over streaming data instead of re-running a saved search. Field names are UDM fields (`security_result.action`), not vendor field names. And the source is not named: the same rule works for any log type whose parser fills these fields, which is why you verify the parser mapping rather than the index name.\n\n### Validating translated detections\n\nA sequence consistent with Google's documentation for a parallel run:\n\n1. **Keep alerts flowing.** Google SecOps can accept alerts from other SIEM systems through SOAR connectors and webhooks, so cases can cover both platforms while you compare.\n2. **Run test** each translated rule against historical data, then compare with what the legacy SIEM found for the same period.\n3. **Retrohunt** with alerting disabled for a wider look back.\n4. **Reconcile counts.** Search and rules differ: repeated fields use \"any\" semantics in search, live rules deduplicate adjacent windows, statistical functions need the `window.` prefix in rules, and test-run deduplication is less restrictive than the live engine.\n5. **Check the data, not just the rule.** A rule that \"misses\" often reflects a parser that did not populate the field.\n\nThe documentation also points to an SPL to YARA-L translator in the Labs area of the console, a starting point rather than a finished rule.\n\n### Access: feature RBAC versus data RBAC\n\nRole-based access control (RBAC) in Google SecOps has two layers. **Feature RBAC** decides which features you can use, for example viewing dashboards versus creating rules, via IAM (Identity and Access Management). **Data RBAC** decides which data you can see using **scopes** assigned to roles. Scoped users see only events, alerts, detections and cases that match their scopes; users with no scopes are global and see everything, and global access overrides scoped access. If a colleague sees an alert you cannot, check scopes before assuming a bug.\n\n### What changes in daily work\n\n| Before | Now |\n|---|---|\n| Learn index and sourcetype names | Learn UDM nouns and event types |\n| Re-run saved searches on a schedule | Continuous rules with windows |\n| Alert in one tool, ticket in another | Alerts group into SOAR cases in the same platform |\n| Hot and cold storage tiers | 12 months searchable by default |\n| Vendor-specific fields per source | One field path across sources |"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Worked End-to-End Investigation: Phishing to Contained Host",
        "content": "Everything so far comes together in one fictional incident at the fictional company corp.example.com. Hostnames, users and addresses use reserved documentation values; the event shapes follow the field mappings in this lesson. The event listing below is illustrative: a real console shows more fields and different column layouts.\n\n### 08:01 to 08:04: the signal\n\nAccountant j.alvarez opens an emailed invoice attachment. Two minutes later an alert arrives in the SOAR case queue: \"Encoded PowerShell launched by an Office application\" on fin-wks-0231. It is the single-event rule from section 8 and carries a risk score of 85. Alert grouping placed it in a new case because no open case shared an entity.\n\n### Step 1: read the rule, then the events\n\nThe Tier-1 analyst opens the alert, reads the rule's `events` section to know exactly what matched, and opens the two UDM events. The parent in `principal.process` is Word, the child in `target.process` is PowerShell with a long `-enc` token. Decoding that base64 text (UTF-16LE) is the next step; the analyst pastes the command line into the Gemini pane for an explanation, then decodes the base64 by hand to confirm the summary rather than trusting it.\n\n### Step 2: pivot with search\n\n```\n// Everything on this host in the 10 minutes after the Word launch\nprincipal.hostname = \"fin-wks-0231.corp.example.com\"\n```\n\nThe timeline shows a `NETWORK_DNS` lookup of `cdn-update-service.example` seconds after PowerShell started, then a `NETWORK_CONNECTION` to 198.51.100.77 on port 443.\n\n### Step 3: threat intelligence lands\n\nAn Applied Threat Intelligence alert for the same domain has been grouped into the case because it shares the host entity. Its priority is Active breach: Mandiant has observed the indicator in an active compromise. The IOC Matches detail names a campaign. In Domain view the analyst sees WHOIS showing recent registration and prevalence of one host in the whole company. The analyst's question is \"outbound only or a real connection?\": the NETWORK_CONNECTION event answers it. Traffic left the network.\n\n### Step 4: scope and playbook containment\n\n```\n// Who else asked for this domain in the last 12 months? (retroactive scope)\nmetadata.event_type = \"NETWORK_DNS\"\nnetwork.dns.questions.name = \"cdn-update-service.example\"\n```\n\nThe search returns one host only. The playbook attached to this alert type has already enriched the entities and queried VirusTotal for the domain. It now creates a **Pending Action** assigned to the Tier-2 role: isolate fin-wks-0231 using the EDR integration, with a 15-minute response timer. A second step, disable user j.alvarez through the identity integration, waits for approval from the incident lead. The Tier-2 analyst reviews the Insights widget and the Case Wall, approves the isolation, and the remote agent or integration returns a Completed result.\n\n### Step 5: close and improve\n\nThe analyst writes the timeline on the Case Wall, adds an entity comment on the workstation, and closes the case with a malicious reason and a phishing root cause. A detection engineer then runs a retrohunt of a new domain-and-process rule with alerting disabled, checks the volume, and only then enables alerting.\n\n### What the case teaches\n\n| Decision point | Good practice shown |\n|---|---|\n| Starting from the rule | Know what matched and what did not |\n| Principal versus target | Parent Word in principal, child PowerShell in target |\n| Threat intelligence | Priority is context; the connection event decided \"real\" |\n| Scope | One retroactive search answered \"who else\" |\n| Playbook | Human approval for isolation, automation for enrichment |\n| Closure | Reason, root cause and a written timeline |\n| Improvement | Retrohunt before alerting on a new rule |\n\nYour own case will differ in details, but the sequence (rule, events, pivot, intelligence, scope, contain, close, improve) is portable.",
        "codeExample": "// Illustrative UDM timeline for the incident (simplified, fictional values)\n\n08:01:12  metadata.event_type = EMAIL_TRANSACTION\n          network.email.from = \"billing@invoice-portal.example\"\n          network.email.to   = \"j.alvarez@corp.example.com\"\n\n08:02:47  metadata.event_type = PROCESS_LAUNCH            (log type WINDOWS_SYSMON, EventID 1)\n          principal.hostname                = \"fin-wks-0231.corp.example.com\"\n          principal.user.userid             = \"j.alvarez\"\n          principal.process.file.full_path  = \"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\Microsoft Office\\\\root\\\\Office16\\\\WINWORD.EXE\"\n          target.process.file.full_path     = \"C:\\\\Windows\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\"\n          target.process.command_line       = \"powershell.exe -nop -w hidden -enc JABjAGwAaQBlAG4AdAAg...\"\n\n08:02:55  metadata.event_type = NETWORK_DNS\n          principal.hostname = \"fin-wks-0231.corp.example.com\"\n          network.dns.questions.name = \"cdn-update-service.example\"\n\n08:02:56  metadata.event_type = NETWORK_CONNECTION\n          principal.hostname = \"fin-wks-0231.corp.example.com\"\n          target.ip   = \"198.51.100.77\"\n          target.port = 443\n          network.ip_protocol = \"TCP\"\n\n08:04:10  Detection: encoded_powershell_from_office   -> alert, risk_score = 85  -> new case\n08:04:40  Detection: Applied Threat Intelligence (priority Active breach) for cdn-update-service.example -> grouped into the same case (shared host entity)\n08:06:00  Playbook: enrich entities, query VirusTotal, create Pending Action \"Isolate host\" (Tier-2 role, 15 min timer)\n08:11:30  Analyst approves isolation -> action result Completed\n08:30:00  Case closed (reason and root cause chosen from the values configured by your admin)"
      }
